  Feedback and Inquiry Regarding Sabbatical Policy Change

  Dear HR Team,

I hope you are doing well.

I’m writing to share some feedback and seek clarification regarding the recent announcement about the discontinuation of the sabbatical benefit. I fully understand the business context behind the decision and appreciate the company’s transparency during a challenging period.

That said, I would like to raise a concern around the transition rule, specifically for employees whose sabbatical eligibility date falls shortly after January 1, 2027. As an example, employees who became eligible in 2026 are still able to either take the sabbatical or opt for one month’s salary, while those whose eligibility date is just weeks later (e.g., February 2027) are no longer eligible at all. From an employee perspective, this creates a sharp cutoff that may feel difficult to reconcile in terms of fairness, despite being administratively clear.

With that in mind, I wanted to ask whether the company would consider a transitional or prorated approach for employees whose eligibility falls shortly after the cutoff date. One possible idea (offered purely as a suggestion for discussion) could be a gradually reduced compensation alternative, for example:

* Employees eligible in 2027 could receive a partial sabbatical compensation (e.g., 80% of one month’s salary),
* 2028 eligibility at a further reduced rate (e.g., 60%),
* And so on, before fully sunsetting the benefit.

This kind of phased approach might help balance cost control with employees’ long-term expectations, particularly for those who have planned their careers with this benefit in mind over many years of service.

I’d appreciate your thoughts on whether any flexibility or transitional consideration is possible, or if there are additional details around the policy rationale that could help employees better understand the decision.

Thank you very much for taking the time to read this, and for your continued support.

Kind regards,
Jerry Zheng
